WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.000
"The Batman" might just be the darkest big-screen&nbsp;
adventure for the Caped Crusader yet, but what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.040
will younger viewers make of it? Keep watching&nbsp;
for things only adults notice in "The Batman."

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.960
At the onset of "The Batman," we see an armed&nbsp;
robbery and an attempted subway mugging on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:22.400
rainy Gotham evening. In a voiceover, the Caped&nbsp;
Crusader waxes philosophical about his war on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.520
the criminal element, saying, "They think I'm&nbsp;
hiding in the shadows ... but I am the shadows."

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:32.720
For some, Batman's brooding tone and delivery&nbsp;
may call to mind Rorschach's inner monologue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.440
from the opening of "Watchmen" — the 1986&nbsp;
series by Alan Moore and artist Dave Gibbons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.360
often credited with paving the way&nbsp;
for "grimdark" superhero stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:45.120
which was adapted into a 2009&nbsp;
film by director Zack Snyder.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.280
Moore and Gibbons (or Frank Miller, for&nbsp;
that matter) didn't invent hardboiled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.920
detectives who engage in foreboding interior&nbsp;
conversations — that trope dates back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.240
the late 1920s. However, the parallels&nbsp;
between Rorschach — or the archetype he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.560
represents — and this Batman don't end&nbsp;
with the beginnings of their stories.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:07.200
Like Batman, Rorschach is a masked vigilante&nbsp;
who keeps a journal loaded with phrases like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.800
"This city's afraid of me; I've&nbsp;
seen its true face." But unlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.920
most cinematic iterations of Batman, Rorschach has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:19.920
zero qualms about murdering criminals, and his&nbsp;
sense of morality leaves no room for gray areas.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.560
There's some Rorschach in Batman, but there's&nbsp;
also some Rorschach in the Riddler as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.720
And in their use of voiceover, they may&nbsp;
all owe something to "Double Indemnity".

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.480
"It'd begun to rain outside and I watched it&nbsp;
get dark and didn't even turn on the light.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:34.640
That didn't help me either."

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.000
According to widely accepted notions about the&nbsp;
Motion Picture Association's ratings system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:47.120
a PG-13 movie may contain exactly one use of&nbsp;
the infamous f-word. Any more f-bombs than that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.520
kick the film up to an R rating, preventing&nbsp;
unaccompanied minors from buying tickets and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.840
potentially damaging the box office takeaway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.680
especially for a major superhero&nbsp;
blockbuster like "The Batman."

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.120
Generally, superhero movies don't bother cursing&nbsp;
at all or dive headfirst into full-blown R-rated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:09.040
territory, but "The Batman" walks the line. Since&nbsp;
the film has a single precious "f---" to give,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:14.000
you might expect to hear it during an intense&nbsp;
emotional moment. That's not what happens.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.880
Early in the movie, Batman and&nbsp;
the Gotham City Police Department&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:20.000
investigate the scene surrounding the&nbsp;
Riddler's murder of Mayor Don Mitchell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.560
Jr.. Observing that it happens to be&nbsp;
October 31st, Commissioner Pete Savage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.280
turns to the Dark Knight and mockingly&nbsp;
wishes him a "Happy F---ing Halloween!"

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.640
It's as if "The Batman" picks up its one and&nbsp;
only f-bomb and casually tosses it aside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.680
just to get it out of the way. This film&nbsp;
knows it doesn't really need to swear, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.360
that doesn't mean it will let a&nbsp;
perfectly good f-bomb go to waste.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.280
Considering DC has been publishing new Batman&nbsp;
stories on a constant basis since the late 1930s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:53.760
it's pretty wild how filmmakers keep returning&nbsp;
to Frank Miller and David Mazzucchelli's "Batman:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.720
Year One" from 1987 for inspiration. There&nbsp;
are certainly many other Batman comics that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.120
could easily be movies, but Hollywood&nbsp;
really loves "Year One," apparently.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.520
The barebones premise of "Year One" — in&nbsp;
which Batman gets the hang of being Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:11.200
and befriends detective James Gordon&nbsp;
— provides the partial foundation for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:16.160
2005's "Batman Begins." However, "The Batman"&nbsp;
also borrows from Miller and Mazzucchelli,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.720
while managing to avoid retreading director&nbsp;
Christopher Nolan's earlier reboot.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:24.640
Fiercely protective of her friends and&nbsp;
more interested in targeting corrupt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:29.360
elites than thievery for its own sake,&nbsp;
Zoë Kravitz's Selina has more in common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.760
with the Catwoman of "Year One" than any&nbsp;
prior onscreen take on the character.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.080
"Maybe we're not so different.&nbsp;
Who are you under there?"

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:42.400
Meanwhile, the Gotham City of "The Batman"&nbsp;
– excluding an abrasive downtown that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.240
a clear stand-in for Times Square —&nbsp;
resembles Mazzucchelli's worn-down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:50.160
overdeveloped Gotham that recalls the&nbsp;
New York City of the '70s and '80s.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.600
He writes terrible goth poetry;&nbsp;
he's mean to his surrogate parent;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.480
he has inherited responsibilities he wants&nbsp;
nothing to do with. Does that sound like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:06.160
the greatest superhero of all time, or&nbsp;
a self-involved, privileged teenager?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:11.760
If Bruce Wayne was real, don't you think&nbsp;
he'd come off as an angry, arrogant jerk?

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:16.800
Dismissed as an "emo brat" by a The Times critic&nbsp;
who doesn't like this movie, Pattinson's Bruce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:21.120
Wayne is one of the more explicitly flawed&nbsp;
takes on The World's Greatest Detective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:26.480
we've seen committed to screen. He's dismissive&nbsp;
toward the ever-loyal Alfred Pennyworth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:29.920
alienates Selina before the end of&nbsp;
their first reconnaissance mission.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:34.400
Batman wants everyone to fear him,&nbsp;
but cops and Penguin flunkies alike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.800
all laugh right in his face. At one&nbsp;
point, Batman bungles a major clue;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:42.800
at another, a daring rooftop escape&nbsp;
ends in a humiliating crash landing.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:46.320
"Oh you're really not as&nbsp;
smart as I thought you were."&nbsp;

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:51.040
Batman-related media has a habit of telling&nbsp;
us Batman is a normal guy with no superpowers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:55.120
while showing us a character who&nbsp;
via top-tier deductive capacities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:00.960
and an unlimited supply of gadgetry might as well&nbsp;
be considered as omnipotent as his Kryptonian BFF.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.840
Meanwhile, "The Batman" offers us a Dark&nbsp;
Knight who actually is a normal guy with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:11.120
a ton of personality defects, who is also&nbsp;
bad at things he tries to do sometimes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:14.960
He's still got a lot of money, but&nbsp;
there's nothing godlike about this Bat.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:19.600
Sometimes in superhero movie discourse,&nbsp;
folks will use the terms "dark"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:24.560
and "realistic" interchangeably. This is&nbsp;
wrong, and "The Batman" demonstrates why.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:30.160
Compared to Frank Gorshin's definitive performance&nbsp;
in the 1966 "Batman" TV series and Jim Carrey's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:37.120
bonkers take in 1995's "Batman Forever," the&nbsp;
Riddler from "The Batman" is far, far darker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:42.400
Aside from the traditional practice of dispensing&nbsp;
riddles to Batman and the GCPD to make his crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:48.320
sprees more interesting, he has little in common&nbsp;
with his predecessors. This Riddler is much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:54.000
murderous than the Riddlers of yore, to the great&nbsp;
misfortune of several Gotham City public servants.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.920
Paul Dano's Riddler demonstrates&nbsp;
tendencies likely meant as homages to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:02.480
other films — his executions of Commissioner&nbsp;
Savage and District Attorney Gil Colson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:06.560
instantly call to mind "Saw" and its&nbsp;
affiliated series of torture romps.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:12.000
When Batman and the GCPD raid Riddler's apartment,&nbsp;
his piles of journals promptly harken to John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.240
Doe's apartment in "Seven." Riddler even&nbsp;
turns himself over to the authorities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:19.840
just like Doe in David Fincher's&nbsp;
1995 crime-horror masterpiece.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:25.600
This Riddler is darker, but he is not&nbsp;
more realistic. We know this because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.400
John Doe and Jigsaw from "Saw" are also&nbsp;
imaginary characters. They are just as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:35.040
unlikely to exist in the real world as a bank&nbsp;
robber who taunts authorities with gimmicky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:39.040
silly clues and dresses up in a green&nbsp;
tuxedo covered in question marks.

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:45.520
It's probably not realistic to expect the main&nbsp;
character in a major studio blockbuster to express&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:50.160
what socially liberal folks would consider an&nbsp;
enlightened attitude about sex work. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:55.680
Batman doesn't think twice about grilling Selina&nbsp;
— a woman he barely knows — about the possibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.000
that she might have done some escort gigs while&nbsp;
employed at the Iceberg Lounge. At one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.520
Selina reluctantly reveals that&nbsp;
Carmine Falcone is her father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:08.080
not her customer, because it's the only way&nbsp;
to get Batman off her freakin' back about it.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:11.440
But let's consider the counterfactual scenario.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:17.680
What if Carmine was Selina's client? What if&nbsp;
Selina had a whole bunch of former johns? Does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:22.480
Batman bring her to jail at that point?&nbsp;
Is it any of Batman's business at all?

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:27.200
There's definitely a chauvinistic and judgmental&nbsp;
component to Bruce's initial attitude towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:32.160
Selina, which is to be expected — this is a&nbsp;
more immature Batman than we're used to; he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:38.000
been sheltered and insulated by insane amounts of&nbsp;
wealth his whole life. Still, you'd think a guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:43.520
who runs around in a form-fitting animal-themed&nbsp;
outfit wouldn't be this close-minded.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.600
At this moment in superhero movie history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:53.920
we just saw three versions of Spider-Man from&nbsp;
different eras join forces in 2021's "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:59.280
No Way Home," we might be on the cusp of&nbsp;
Professor Charles Xavier joining the MCU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:04.160
after decades as the licensed property of a&nbsp;
different movie studio, and Michael Keaton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:10.400
is returning to fold his decades-old Batman&nbsp;
movies into the DCEU in 2022's "The Flash."

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:14.480
As seemingly all the other superhero&nbsp;
franchises lean into the science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:19.600
fiction aspects of their source materials to&nbsp;
cultivate fan service and corporate synergy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:25.840
this version of Gotham City feels way too&nbsp;
grounded for any of that wacky nonsense.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:27.440
"Whoa! Take it easy, sweetheart."

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:30.880
Not only are there no references&nbsp;
to other DC superheroes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:35.840
"The Batman" doesn't even have quasi-fantastic&nbsp;
elements like the high-speed military tank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:40.080
Batmobile or the League of Shadows from the&nbsp;
Christopher Nolan trilogy — the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:45.520
standard setter for "gritty realism" in Batman&nbsp;
movies. In fact, Matt Reeves told Esquire,

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:49.680
"I respect that the DC Universe&nbsp;
has become an extended universe and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:54.800
all the movies were kind of connected. But another&nbsp;
Batman film, it shouldn’t have to carry the weight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.640
of connecting the characters from all those&nbsp;
other movies. I didn’t want them in there."

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:03.760
Who the heck knows what DC has planned, but if&nbsp;
ninjas are too magic-adjacent for this Batman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:07.840
don't expect to see him hanging out&nbsp;
with Kryptonians, Amazon Princesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:11.680
intergalactic peacekeepers, or&nbsp;
folks who can run through time.

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:18.080
To some extent since 2008's "Iron Man," and&nbsp;
to a much greater extent since 2012's "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:23.600
Avengers," basically every superhero movie is&nbsp;
either on the trajectory toward a big team-up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:28.000
about a big team-up, or dealing&nbsp;
with the aftermath of a big team-up.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.760
Almost all of these films take place&nbsp;
in a shared universe that includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:36.640
a bunch of other films. Many superheroes talk as&nbsp;
if they're being written by someone hired to mimic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:41.680
Joss Whedon's style circa the early 2010s;&nbsp;
even the ones who don't — and that includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:46.880
denizens of the "adult" and "serious"&nbsp;
comic book movies — tell tons of jokes.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:50.560
"You're gonna do that thing where you&nbsp;
open up a weird-ass case of torture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:55.040
devices while inexplicably detailing your&nbsp;
master plan and how I don't fit into it."

00:09:55.040 --> 00:10:00.240
"The Batman," however, is not funny. The&nbsp;
only other movie it indirectly advertises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.880
is its own yet-to-be-officially greenlit&nbsp;
sequel. "The Batman" only introduces one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:08.320
surprise character from the comics who&nbsp;
isn't in the trailers. There's not even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:11.520
much in the way of obvious potential&nbsp;
for toy licensing in this movie.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:16.080
Despite its arguably messy execution of&nbsp;
too much story, "The Batman" feels like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:21.200
an emphatic step away from the MCU paradigm.&nbsp;
Assuming it's the first movie in a series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:24.880
hopefully it carries on in that&nbsp;
direction. The MCU's good and fine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:29.840
but we already have one of those. We also&nbsp;
already have a DC cinematic universe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:35.760
very much wishes it was the MCU. Maybe "The&nbsp;
Batman" will be the start of a third thing.

